+---
+id: dl-001-stable-task-ids-via-a-per-list-sequential-counter
+type: decision-log
+title: "Stable task ids via a per-list sequential counter"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Tasks were identified by title alone, so renaming a task broke anything holding a reference to it. The
+app needs an identifier that survives a rename. The `determinism` and `r3-no-clock-or-randomness`
+directives forbid wall-clock reads and randomness anywhere under `src/domain/`, which rules out the
+usual choices for generating an id (`Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`, `Math.random()`).
+
+## Decision
+
+Each `TaskList` keeps a private counter, starting at 1, that is never reset or decremented. `add`
+assigns the next task the id `t${n}` and advances the counter, so ids are unique for the life of the
+list and are never reused, even after the task they named is removed. `createTask` now takes this id as
+a parameter instead of generating it, keeping `src/domain/task.ts` a pure function of its inputs.
+
+The list is now keyed by id internally (`Map<id, Task>`) instead of by title, with a linear scan over
+values to resolve a title to a task (titles still have to be unique, enforced on `add` and `rename`).
+`find`, `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag`, `untag` and `remove` resolve their first argument as
+either an id or a title (id checked first), and a new `get(id)` method looks a task up by id only.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers can hold onto a task's `id` across a rename instead of re-finding it by title.
+- The sequential id format (`t1`, `t2`, ...) is deterministic and is pinned by a test; it is also, as a
+  side effect, predictable and reveals how many tasks a list has ever held. Acceptable for this
+  in-memory, single-process list; would need revisiting if ids are ever exposed across untrusted
+  boundaries.
+- `find`-family methods now do a linear scan when given a title (previously an O(1) map lookup); fine at
+  this list's expected size.
